---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const semCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Semantics: literal meaning', note: 'What words and sentences MEAN independent of context: bachelor = unmarried male; "Cats kill mice" means what it means in any universe. Truth conditions: a sentence\'s meaning is when it would be true.' },
    { math: '2 · Pragmatics: meaning in use', note: '"Can you pass the salt?" — the literal answer is "yes" (and no salt moves). Grice\'s maxims (be relevant, informative, truthful, brief) explain how we mean MORE than we say: flouting a maxim sends a signal.' },
    { math: '3 · Implicature: the implied layer', note: '"Some students passed" implies "not all did" — though "some" doesn\'t strictly exclude "all". Implicatures are cancellable ("some — in fact all — passed") — that cancellability is the diagnostic.' },
    { math: '4 · Speech acts and indirectness', note: 'Requests are disguised as questions everywhere; politeness systems (Brown & Levinson) manage face. Cross-cultural pragmatics: "no" means different things in different cultures — pragmatics is where intercultural miscommunication lives.' },
  ],
};

const semQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: '"Some students passed" implying "not all passed" is…', choices: ['an entailment', 'a scalar implicature', 'a presupposition', 'a lie'], answer: 1, why: 'Some is logically compatible with all — the not-all reading is inferred from the maxim of quantity and is cancellable ("some — in fact all — passed"), which entailments never are.' },
    { q: '"Have you stopped cheating on exams?" smuggles in the claim that…', choices: ['you never cheated', 'you once cheated', 'exams matter', 'nothing — it is neutral'], answer: 1, why: 'Presupposition: the question is only felicitous if you did cheat, so the loaded content travels below the main question — the lawyer\'s classic device.' },
    { q: 'A reference letter that reads only "Mr Smith\'s English is excellent and his attendance is regular" communicates by…', choices: ['presupposition', 'flouting the maxim of quantity', 'anaphora', 'deixis'], answer: 1, why: 'The letter is obviously insufficient for a reference — the shortage itself signals the intended meaning (a weak candidate). Saying less than required is information.' },
    { q: '"Can you pass the salt?" is literally a question about ability but functions as…', choices: ['a promise', 'an indirect request', 'a warning', 'an entailment'], answer: 1, why: 'Speech-act theory: the utterance performs a request via its literal question form, and hearers recover the request through relevance — literal answer "yes" would itself require explanation.' },
  ],
};

const semSenseMatch = {
  "prompt": "Frege's split at work — pair the expression with what it demonstrates:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "reference",
      "right": "what the word points at in the world"
    },
    {
      "left": "sense",
      "right": "the mode of presentation — the description under which Venus is picked out"
    },
    {
      "left": "'the morning star IS the evening star'",
      "right": "an informative identity: same referent, different senses"
    },
    {
      "left": "'unicorn'",
      "right": "sense without a referent — meaning with nothing pointed at"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Meaning is not just the thing pointed at — which is why identity claims can inform and empty names can still mean."
};

const semLexBuckets = {
  "prompt": "Testable relations in the lexicon — file each:",
  "buckets": [
    "hyponymy — class membership",
    "meronymy — part–whole",
    "componential analysis and its leaks"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "a spaniel is a hyponym of dog, which is a hyponym of animal",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "wheel to car",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "bachelor = [male][adult][unmarried]",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "a 'bachelor' pope or a stranded male seal strains the analysis",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "the relation dictionaries and ontologies are built from",
      "bucket": 0
    }
  ],
  "why": "Word meaning is partly world knowledge, not just features — that is where the classic puzzle leaks."
};

const semEntailJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "'Every student passed' entails 'Some student passed'.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "True in every situation the first is true — entailment needs no speaker."
    },
    {
      "s": "'No student passed' contradicts it.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The two cannot be true in the same world."
    },
    {
      "s": "'Some students failed' contradicts 'Every student passed'.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "It is compatible with either — a third relation, weaker than contradiction."
    },
    {
      "s": "'Cats kill mice' means different things in different contexts.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Its truth conditions stay fixed across contexts — that fixity is what pragmatics operates against."
    }
  ]
};

const semScopeCompare = {
  "prompt": "Compositionality's edge case — drag the divider between the readings:",
  "before": {
    "label": "Reading 1 — for each man, some possibly-different woman",
    "text": "'Every' takes scope over 'a' — the default reading."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "Reading 2 — one particular woman for all men",
    "text": "'A' takes wide scope — same words, two scope assignments."
  },
  "why": "Exactly parallel to the structural ambiguity the syntax lesson found in trees, but computed over meanings."
};

const semMaximSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this maxims summary is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Quantity: be informative — say as much as the exchange requires.",
    "Quality: be truthful.",
    "Relation: be relevant.",
    "Manner: be clear and brief.",
    "A maxim violation must stay hidden for the message to land."
  ],
  "errorLine": 4,
  "correction": "Flouting is open violation the hearer cannot miss — assume cooperation and the violation itself becomes the message.",
  "why": "Irony and metaphor flout quality ('Lovely weather' in a downpour): meaning is recovered by assuming truthfulness at a higher level."
};

const semScaleOrder = {
  "prompt": "Ordered scales under the inference — arrange weakest to strongest:",
  "items": [
    "some (of the students)",
    "most (of the students)",
    "all (of the students)"
  ],
  "why": "Saying the weaker term implicates the stronger was not available — the same logic orders possibly–probably–certainly and warm–hot. The diagnostic is cancellability: the vanishing-without-contradiction inference that law, contracts and journalism all lean on."
};

const semPresupJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "'John's brother is tall' presupposes that John has a brother.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Content beneath the main assertion — it travels whether or not the assertion is agreed with."
    },
    {
      "s": "'John's brother isn't tall' no longer presupposes the brother exists.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Presupposition survives negation — projection is the test."
    },
    {
      "s": "Presuppositions project through questions and negation; implicatures cancel.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The field's cleanest separation of meaning layers."
    },
    {
      "s": "The loaded question is a modern rhetorical invention.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "The rhetorical uses are ancient — the frame that contains the assumption before the debate begins."
    }
  ]
};

const semAnaphoraBranch = {
  "root": "One pronoun, two candidates — walk the resolution.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "'Mary told Susan that her car was ready.' Which owner does 'her' prefer?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Mary — the subject",
          "next": "pref",
          "feedback": "Preference runs the other way: 'her' leans to the nearer antecedent."
        },
        {
          "label": "Susan — the nearer name",
          "next": "pref",
          "feedback": "Right as a default — but preference is not grammar."
        }
      ]
    },
    "pref": {
      "text": "'Mary told Susan that she herself was ready' flips it; 'Mary said to Susan that her flight left at six' could genuinely be either — real conversation resolves it with stress, context or repair ('her — my — flight'). This is anaphora: referring back with reduced material.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Now the subtler layer",
          "next": "bridge"
        }
      ]
    },
    "bridge": {
      "text": "'We stopped for lunch. The waiter was rude.' Nothing introduced a waiter — bridging manufactures one from the inference: restaurants have waiters. Every sentence of connected discourse leans on these inferences.",
      "choices": []
    }
  },
  "why": "The context does the work the words omit — and machines that resolve 'it' correctly are doing pragmatics, while the ones that fail are doing dictionary lookup."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/intermediate/semantics-and-pragmatics">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Linguistics lesson. The foundational lesson How language
    works introduced the field's layers, and the sibling lessons on morphology and
    syntax explained how words and sentences are built. This lesson asks the harder
    question that building leaves open: what do they mean — first out of context, and
    then in it, where most of communication actually happens.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>split meaning into its two layers: stable, context-free semantics and meaning-in-use pragmatics;</li>
    <li>test word meanings with sense, reference and hyponymy, and say where componential analysis leaks;</li>
    <li>use truth conditions and entailment as the logical baseline of sentence meaning;</li>
    <li>apply Grice's maxims to explain irony, metaphor and the indirect request;</li>
    <li>run the cancellability test to separate an implicature from an entailment, and spot a presupposition that survives negation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Syntax and morphology explained how sentences are built. This lesson asks the harder
    question: what do they <em>mean</em>? The field splits the labour in two.
    <strong>Semantics</strong> handles stable, context-free meaning — what a word or
    sentence means regardless of who says it where. <strong>Pragmatics</strong> handles
    meaning in use — what the speaker manages to mean <em>with</em> that sentence, in this
    context: implication, politeness, irony, indirect requests. The two layers answer the
    two halves of the salt question: "Can you pass the salt?" <em>means</em> (semantically)
    a question about ability, and <em>does</em> (pragmatically) a request.
  </p>
  <p>
    The gap between what is said and what is meant is not noise around the signal — it is
    most of communication, and it is <strong>systematic</strong>: governed by rules you can
    state, test, and break deliberately. This lesson builds the semantics toolkit first
    (sense, reference, truth conditions, entailment), then the pragmatics one (Grice's
    maxims, implicature, speech acts, presupposition), and ends where the field is now:
    arguing about how much of meaning is computed from context in real time.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="truth conditions, Grice's maxims, implicature, speech acts" widget="equationStep" config={semCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four panels move steadily outward from the
    sentence: panel 1 fixes the context-free meaning, panel 2 brings the speaker in,
    panel 3 recovers meaning the speaker did not say, and panel 4 acts on the world.
    Watch the salt question travel through all four — its truth-conditional content
    never changes, yet almost nothing a dinner-table host actually communicates is
    inside panel 1.
  </p>

  <h2>Word meaning: sense, reference and the lexicon</h2>
  <p>
    A word's meaning has at least two distinct parts. <strong>Reference</strong> is what
    the word points at in the world: "the morning star" and "the evening star" both
    refer to Venus, yet they differ in <strong>sense</strong> — the mode of presentation,
    the description under which Venus is picked out. The distinction (Frege's, 1892) does
    real work: it explains why informative identities exist at all ("the morning star IS
    the evening star" tells you something, though both names refer alike) and why words
    with no referent ("unicorn") still have meaning.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each expression with what it shows" widget="match" config={semSenseMatch} />
  <p>
    Within the lexicon, meanings relate in patterned ways that you can test. Synonymy
    (interchangeability with meaning kept), antonymy (opposites that share a frame:
    hot/cold), <strong>hyponymy</strong> (class membership: a spaniel is a hyponym of dog,
    which is a hyponym of animal — the relation dictionaries and ontologies are built
    from), meronymy (part–whole: wheel to car). Componential analysis tries to decompose
    meanings into features — bachelor = [male][adult][unmarried] — powerful and famously
    leaky (a "bachelor" pope or a stranded male seal strains the analysis; the classic
    puzzle shows word meaning is partly world knowledge, not just features).
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each relation by the pattern it runs on" widget="sort-buckets" config={semLexBuckets} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if two expressions refer to the same thing, they
  mean the same thing." The morning star and the evening star are both Venus, yet the
  identity claim between them is genuinely informative — the senses differ, the modes
  of presentation, even though the reference is one planet. Meaning is not just the
  thing pointed at; that is also why words with no referent at all, like "unicorn",
  still mean something.</p>

  <h2>Sentence meaning: truth conditions and entailment</h2>
  <p>
    The formal semantics tradition (building on Frege, Tarski and Montague) defines a
    sentence's meaning by its <strong>truth conditions</strong>: a description of the
    situations in which the sentence would be true. "Cats kill mice" is true exactly in
    worlds where cats kill mice — that is its literal content, and it stays fixed across
    contexts. From truth conditions flow the entailment relations that logic cares about:
    "Every student passed" <strong>entails</strong> "Some student passed" (true in every
    situation the first is true); "No student passed" contradicts it; "Some students
    failed" is compatible with either. Entailment is meaning without any speaker
    involved — which is precisely what makes it the baseline against which pragmatics
    operates.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — the logical baseline" widget="judge" config={semEntailJudge} />
  <p>
    Compositionality (the <strong>Fregean principle</strong>) underwrites the whole
    enterprise: the meaning of a complex expression is a function of the meanings of its
    parts and how they are combined. That principle is why you can understand sentences
    you have never heard, and its edge cases are where the interesting failures live:
    quantifier scope. "Every man loves a woman" has two readings (for each man some
    possibly-different woman; or one particular woman for all men) — same words, two
    scope assignments, exactly parallel to the structural ambiguity the syntax lesson
    found in trees, but computed over meanings.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag the divider — one sentence, two scopes" widget="compare" config={semScopeCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> semantics fixed the context-free core — truth conditions,
    entailment, compositionality — with no speaker anywhere in the picture. Now a
    speaker arrives, and the machinery that lets them mean more than they say needs
    rules of its own.
  </p>

  <h2>Pragmatics: the cooperative principle</h2>
  <p>
    Now the speaker arrives, and meaning changes. H. Paul Grice's <strong>cooperative
    principle</strong> (1967 lectures, published 1975) proposes that conversation runs on
    an assumption of cooperation, cashed out as four maxims: be <strong>informative</strong>
    (quantity), be <strong>truthful</strong> (quality), be <strong>relevant</strong>,
    be <strong>clear and brief</strong> (manner). The power of the scheme is what happens
    when a maxim is <em>flouted</em> — openly violated in a way the hearer cannot miss.
    Assume the speaker is still cooperating, and the violation itself becomes a message.
    Irony flouts quality ("Lovely weather" in a downpour — meaning is recovered by
    assuming truthfulness at a higher level); metaphor flouts quality the same way;
    and the classic quantity example is the academic reference letter that reads, in
    full, "Mr Smith's English is excellent and his attendance is regular" — obviously
    insufficient for the genre, and therefore understood as a devastating "not
    recommended".
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the line Grice would correct" widget="spot-error" config={semMaximSpot} />
  <p>
    The indirect-request machinery runs on the same engine. "Can you pass the salt?" is
    literally a question about ability; hearing it as a request requires no separate
    rule — it falls out of relevance (why would I ask about your arm? it's on the table).
    Politeness systems regulate this layer: Brown and Levinson's account (1987) treats
    indirectness as <strong>face management</strong> — requests threaten the hearer's
    autonomy ("negative face"), so the threat is cushioned in proportion to social
    distance and power. That is why politeness formulas differ across languages while
    the underlying problem is identical, and why Japanese honorific grammar builds the
    social hierarchy into the verb morphology itself: the pragmatics is inside the
    grammar, not beside it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "indirectness is sloppy or evasive communication."
  It is neither: the indirect request falls out of relevance by an inferable path, and
  politeness systems cushion the threat to the hearer's face in proportion to social
  distance and power. Indirectness is a rule-governed layer — often the politest and
  most efficient way to get the salt passed.</p>

  <h2>Implicature: the cancellable layer</h2>
  <p>
    Grice named the inferred messages <strong>implicatures</strong>, and gave them a
    precise signature: unlike entailments, they are <strong>cancellable</strong>. "Some
    students passed" implicates "not all did" — but the implicature vanishes without
    contradiction in "Some — in fact all — students passed", while "All students passed"
    can never entail "not all". The canonical cases are <strong>scalar</strong>: ordered
    scales (some–most–all, possibly–probably–certainly, warm–hot) where saying the weaker
    term implicates the stronger was not available. The diagnostic matters practically:
    it separates the logical core of meaning from the inferred wrapper, which is exactly
    the separation law, contracts and journalism all lean on ("some employees" is not
    a false statement if all employees did the thing).
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="order the scale the inference rides on" widget="order" config={semScaleOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>Presupposition</strong> is the smuggling channel alongside. "Have you stopped
    cheating on exams?" presupposes you once did; "John's brother is tall" presupposes
    John has a brother — content that travels beneath the main assertion and survives
    under negation ("John's brother isn't tall" still presupposes the brother exists).
    Presuppositions behave differently from implicatures (they project through questions
    and negation; implicatures cancel) — the technical distinction is the field's cleanest
    separation of meaning layers, and the rhetorical uses are ancient: the loaded
    question, the frame that contains the assumption before the debate begins.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — what travels beneath" widget="judge" config={semPresupJudge} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> pragmatics is not "what people really mean deep down"
  in a fuzzy sense — it is a computational layer with rules, diagnostics and failures.
  The cancellability test, the presupposition projection tests, the maxim labels: these
  are instruments, and using them separates analysis from intuition.</p>

  <h2>Deixis: where context is wired into grammar</h2>
  <p>
    Some words are meaningless without context — not vague, but structurally dependent on
    it. <strong>Deictics</strong> anchor utterances to the speech situation: I/you (person —
    "I" refers to whoever says it), here/there, this/that (place), now/then, yesterday
    (time), and tense itself. The philosophical point: "I am here now" is trivially true
    for anyone, anywhere, ever — yet unparaphrasable by any context-free sentence; some
    meaning can only be computed relative to an anchor. Languages differ in how much they
    grammaticalise the anchor: Japanese and Spanish encode social distance even in "you";
    many languages make demonstratives encode distance from the speaker precisely (this /
    that-near-you / that-far). Deixis is the seam between the two halves of the lesson —
    semantically, these words have schematic meanings; pragmatically, they are resolved in
    milliseconds by whoever is speaking — and every language forces its speakers through
    that resolution dozens of times a minute.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the maxims explain how speakers mean more than they say,
    and deixis shows context wired into grammar itself. One gap remains: reference in
    running text — how pronouns and definite descriptions find their owners.
  </p>

  <h2>Reference and anaphora: pointing without names</h2>
  <p>
    Watch what pronouns do. "Mary told Susan that her car was ready" — whose car? "Her"
    prefers the nearer antecedent, but preference is not grammar: "Mary told Susan that
    she herself was ready" flips it; "Mary said to Susan that her flight left at six"
    could genuinely be either, and real conversation resolves it with stress, context or
    repair ("her — my — flight"). This is <strong>anaphora</strong>: referring back with
    reduced material, resolved by inference. The layer below it is subtler still:
    <strong>bridging</strong> — "We stopped for lunch. The waiter was rude." Nothing
    introduced a waiter; the inference (restaurants have waiters) manufactures one. Every
    sentence of connected discourse leans on these inferences, which is why isolated
    sentences feel unnatural and why dialogue is efficient: the context does the work the
    words omit. Reference resolution is also where semantics demonstrably becomes
    computation — machines that resolve "it" correctly are doing pragmatics, and the
    ones that fail are doing dictionary lookup.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="resolve the pronoun — walk the inference" widget="branch" config={semAnaphoraBranch} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>"Some students passed" is heard as implying "not all passed". What kind of inference is that, and how do you prove it?</li>
    <li>"Have you stopped cheating on exams?" smuggles in a claim. What is the device called, and why does it survive negation?</li>
    <li>A reference letter reads only "Mr Smith's English is excellent and his attendance is regular". What mechanism lets two sentences say so much?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) A scalar implicature — "some" is logically
    compatible with "all"; the not-all reading is cancellable ("some — in fact all —
    passed"), which entailments never are. (2) Presupposition: the question is only
    felicitous if you once cheated, and presupposed content projects through questions
    and negation, unlike implicatures. (3) Flouting the maxim of quantity: the letter
    is obviously insufficient for the genre, and the shortage itself is read as the
    message — a weak candidate.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Semantics handles context-free meaning; pragmatics handles meaning in use.</li>
    <li>Reference is what a word points at; sense is the mode of presentation — both are needed.</li>
    <li>A sentence's literal meaning is its truth conditions; entailments flow without any speaker involved.</li>
    <li>Flouting a maxim openly is itself a message — the engine behind irony, metaphor and indirect requests.</li>
    <li>Implicatures are cancellable; presuppositions project through negation — the two tests separate the layers.</li>
    <li>Deictics anchor utterances to the speech situation and are resolved dozens of times a minute.</li>
    <li>Anaphora and bridging show that connected discourse runs on manufactured inference.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced lessons Sociolinguistics and Psycholinguistics and acquisition take
    the two halves further — politeness and culture-specific indirectness into the
    social world, the late-developing scalar implicature into the child's mind. The
    sibling lesson Syntax trees and rules is the natural reread: the trees this lesson
    kept referencing are where its meanings are built.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The modern research front has three landmarks. <strong>Relevance theory</strong>
    (Sperber and Wilson, 1986) argued that the four maxims collapse into one principle —
    hearers assume an utterance is relevant enough to be worth their processing effort —
    and that all the Gricean effects fall out of that single computation; it remains the
    strongest minimalist rival, and the two frameworks still divide the field.
    <strong>Acquisition</strong>: children compute entailments early but standard scalar
    implicatures late — experimental work puts reliable "some means not-all" readings
    around age five and later — one of the cleanest examples of a pragmatic inference
    being a learnable, maturing computation rather than mere logic. And
    <strong>computation</strong>: literal semantics is the easy part for language models;
    implicature, irony, register and culture-specific indirectness are where they fail
    recognisably — a research frontier that doubles as a proof of Grice's thesis, since
    the layer he formalised is precisely the layer machines have to be taught
    separately. Cross-cultural pragmatics completes the warning: directness, silence,
    refusing an offer and even "yes" itself (which may mean "I hear you" in some
    conversation cultures) are regulated locally — meaning-in-use is the layer where
    intercultural miscommunication actually lives.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two frameworks close the gap between the formal and the human sides.
    <strong>Frame semantics</strong> (Charles Fillmore) argues word meanings are windows
    onto structured scenarios: "buy", "sell", "pay" and "cost" all evoke one commercial
    frame (buyer, seller, goods, money) and differ in which participant they spotlight —
    which explains why "I bought it from him" and "he sold it to me" are two views of one
    event, and why synonyms that share denotation can differ completely in the frame they
    impose (the framing analysis of this lesson's figures-of-speech sibling, rebuilt as a
    theory of the lexicon). <strong>Computational semantics</strong> turns the truth-
    conditional toolkit into software: parsing to logical forms, theorem provers checking
    entailment (the technology behind search relevance and database question-answering),
    and distributional methods learning word meaning from co-occurrence statistics —
    which capture sense relations surprisingly well and truth conditions not at all, an
    instructive partial success that maps exactly onto the semantics/pragmatics split
    this lesson began with. Meaning, it turns out, is not one problem: it is a stack,
    and this lesson has named the layers.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Grice, "Logic and Conversation" (1975); Sperber &amp; Wilson, Relevance (1986); Levinson, Pragmatics (1983); Brown &amp; Levinson, Politeness (1987). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: semantics and pragmatics" widget="quiz" config={semQuizCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> every explanation rests on one of the two
    diagnostics — cancellability separates implicature from entailment, and projection
    through negation identifies presupposition. If you can say which test certifies
    each answer, you have the instruments, not just the vocabulary.
  </p>
</TopicPage>
